      handoutIntro:'A reminder to discuss with your PD team. Your individual instructions come first.',handoutHeads:['Before your visit','During placement','Healing and starting dialysis','Looking after the catheter','Call your PD team immediately'],placementSummary:'The spot is marked and the skin is cleaned and draped. The area is numbed layer by layer. A needle and a soft guidewire are used to guide a soft tube into the belly space, with warmed fluid run in first to make room. A short tunnel under the skin brings the tube out through a downward-facing exit site, and two soft cuffs anchor it. Fluid is run in and out to check it works. You are usually awake with the area numbed; tell the team about pain.',checklistIntro:'For routine care after healing, once your PD nurse has trained you. For a newly placed catheter, follow the early dressing instructions below.',checklistItems:['Wash and dry your hands before care.','Look at the exit site and nearby skin each day.','Follow the cleaning schedule and use only the products your team recommends.','Use any prescribed ointment exactly as taught.','Keep the tube secured so it cannot tug; avoid tight clothing rubbing the site.','Know your PD team’s daytime and after-hours numbers.'],earlyCare:'While the site is healing',caregiverIntro:'You do not need to do everything. Agree with your loved one and the PD nurse on how you can help.',caregiverItems:['Ask what help feels useful; listen and write down questions.','Wash and dry your hands before helping with care.','Help look at the exit site without pulling the tube or disturbing a new dressing.','Only carry out care or exchanges after the PD team has trained you.','Keep supplies ready and support the team’s care plan.','Call the PD team immediately for the warning signs below.'],noteEmergency:'If you cannot reach the PD team promptly, seek urgent medical care. For severe illness, use your local emergency service.',
      phases:[
        {name:'Meet your catheter',sub:'A small tube with a big purpose',category:'Getting familiar',title:'Meet your PD catheter',description:'Peritoneal dialysis uses the lining inside your belly — the peritoneum — to clean your blood. A soft silicone tube lets dialysis fluid flow in and out through that space. The drawings below show exactly where it runs: through the belly wall, along a tunnel under the skin, and down into the pelvis. Explore each part at your own pace.',visual:'anatomy',statuses:[['heart','A new routine, gently','It’s natural to have questions. You can learn a little at a time, and come back to any part of this.'],['leaf','Designed to stay with you','Two soft cuffs anchor the tube as your own tissue grows into them. Only a short length stays outside, and it tucks under clothing.'],['people','You’ll have support','Your PD nurse will teach you to use and care for the catheter before you do anything on your own.']],caregiver:'Sit together and use the five buttons under the drawing. Ask what feels unclear and write those questions down for the PD nurse.',question:'What is the catheter there to do?',answers:['Let dialysis fluid move in and out of my belly space','Go into my stomach to carry food'],correct:0,yes:'That’s right. It carries dialysis fluid into and out of the belly space. It does not go anywhere near your stomach or your food.',no:'A helpful distinction: the tube sits in the belly space around your organs, not inside your stomach. It carries dialysis fluid only.'},
        {name:'Before your procedure',sub:'Marking the spot, together',category:'Preparing',title:'Choosing where the tube comes out',description:'Where your exit site sits matters for years, so it is chosen carefully and marked before the day — usually with you sitting or standing, in your normal clothes. The team keeps it away from your belt line, from skin folds and from old scars, and places it where you can actually see and reach it.',visual:'prepare',points:['markingInstructions','fastingInstructions','medicationInstructions','bowelInstructions','arrivalInstructions'],statuses:[['clipboard','Your own preparation plan','Fasting and medicine instructions depend on your health and your center. Follow yours, not a friend’s.'],['message','Tell your team','Mention allergies, blood-thinning medicines, past belly operations, and where your waistband normally sits.'],['people','Plan some company','Ask who may come with you and arrange your trip home before the day.']],caregiver:'Help gather the medicine list, written instructions, and questions. Confirm the transport plan, and remind the team about the clothing your loved one usually wears so the marking suits real life.',question:'Why is the exit site marked while you sit or stand?',answers:['To match a standard position for everyone','So it avoids your belt line and skin folds in real life'],correct:1,yes:'Exactly. Lying flat hides the folds and the waistband. Marking upright puts the exit somewhere that works when you are living your normal day.',no:'There is no standard spot. Marking is done upright so the site avoids your own belt line and skin folds when you sit and stand.'},
        {name:'Keeping you comfortable',sub:'Numbing & relaxation',category:'Your comfort',title:'Your comfort matters',description:'The area is numbed in layers: skin first, then a little deeper each time, down through the fat and muscle sheath to the lining of the belly. The first injection stings briefly. After that you may feel pressure or tugging, but not sharp pain. Light sedation may also be offered.',visual:'comfort',note:['You can speak up at any point.','Tell the team if you feel sharp pain, worry, or need something explained again. Nothing about this has to be endured quietly.'],statuses:[['heart','What you may feel','A brief sting, then pressure or tugging as the tube is guided. Sharp pain is not expected — say so if you feel it.'],['leaf','Awake or lightly drowsy','This is usually done with local anaesthetic rather than a general anaesthetic. Sedation is optional and depends on your needs and your team’s plan.'],['people','Someone is looking after you','A member of the team stays with you and checks how you are feeling throughout.']],caregiver:'Tell the team beforehand about your loved one’s worries, hearing needs, or preferred language. Ask whether you can be nearby.',question:'If I feel sharp pain, what should I do?',answers:['Tell the team right away','Stay quiet and wait for it to finish'],correct:0,yes:'Yes. Say so immediately — more local anaesthetic can be given, and comfort is part of doing this well.',no:'You do not have to stay quiet. Sharp pain means the team should add more numbing. Tell them right away.'},
        {name:'Placing the catheter',sub:'Follow the eight stages',category:'The procedure',title:'The bedside placement, stage by stage',description:'This is the percutaneous method: no large incision, and usually no general anaesthetic. A needle and a soft guidewire guide the tube into place through a small opening. Follow all eight stages below, or play them in sequence. Instruments are drawn as simple shapes, and nothing graphic is shown.',visual:'placement',statuses:[['leaf','A small opening','A short opening, not a large cut. The tube is guided in over a wire rather than through open surgery.'],['clock','One stage at a time','Ask your team how long your own procedure and the observation afterwards are likely to take.'],['shield','A check before you leave','Fluid is run in and drained out to confirm the catheter works before the dressing goes on.']],caregiver:'This is a simplified map so your loved one knows what is happening, not instructions for carrying it out. Ask the team to explain anything the drawing leaves out.',question:'Why is warm fluid run into the belly before the tube goes in?',answers:['To lift the bowel gently away and make a clear space','To warm the patient up'],correct:0,yes:'That’s right. The fluid floats the bowel away from the entry point, which makes room for the catheter and is an important safety step.',no:'It is a safety step, not a warming one: the fluid lifts the bowel away from the entry point so there is a clear space for the tube.'},
        {name:'Right after placement',sub:'Rest, check & recover',category:'Early recovery',title:'Time to rest and check in',description:'Before you go anywhere, the team runs fluid in and drains it out again to confirm the catheter flows freely in both directions. Mild soreness around the site is common for a few days. Tell the team about pain that is strong, getting worse, or different from what you were told to expect.',visual:'recovery',points:['dischargeInstructions','dressingInstructions'],statuses:[['heart','A little soreness is possible','Aching around the site for a few days is usual. Strong or worsening pain is not — say so.'],['clipboard','Before heading home','Make sure you leave knowing your dressing plan, your follow-up date, and who to call.'],['people','A supported trip home','If you had sedation, follow the team’s travel and supervision instructions and do not drive yourself.']],caregiver:'Listen to the discharge instructions together and write them down. Help with the journey home, and make sure the after-hours number is saved in a phone before you leave.',question:'What should I know before leaving?',answers:['Only the date of my next visit','My care plan, my follow-up date, and who to call'],correct:1,yes:'Yes. Ask for written instructions and a number for help, including after hours.',no:'The date matters, and so do your care plan and your contact numbers. Ask for all three before you leave.'},
        {name:'Giving your body time',sub:'The break-in period',category:'Healing',title:'Let your catheter settle in',description:'The break-in period is not just waiting. Your own tissue is growing into the two fabric cuffs, and that ingrowth is what anchors the tube and seals the tunnel against infection. Until it happens, the catheter is only resting in place — which is why it must not be tugged.',visual:'healing',points:['dialysisStartInstructions','dressingInstructions','activityInstructions'],statuses:[['clock','At least 2 weeks, when possible','ISPD guidance favours a planned break-in of at least two weeks. Your team chooses your start date.'],['shield','Protect the dressing','Keep it dry and undisturbed. Ask your team for help if it is wet, dirty, loose, or blood-soaked.'],['leaf','Be gentle with yourself','Avoid straining and heavy lifting. Follow your constipation prevention plan — straining puts pressure on a healing site.']],caregiver:'Take on the lifting and the chores that involve straining. Keep the follow-up plan somewhere visible, and leave early dressing changes to the PD team.',question:'When should I begin dialysis exchanges?',answers:['As soon as I feel ready','Only when my PD team tells me to'],correct:1,yes:'Exactly. Feeling well and being healed are different things. Your team chooses the safe start date and teaches you the plan.',no:'Feeling ready does not mean the cuffs have sealed. Start only when your team says so.'},
        {name:'Living with your catheter',sub:'Small habits, daily care',category:'Life at home',title:'Build a gentle care routine',description:'Once the site has healed, your PD nurse will teach you to look after it. A settled exit site is calm, dry, and unremarkable — that is what you are checking for each day. Keep the tube secured so it cannot tug, and choose clothing that does not rub across it.',visual:'living',points:['dailyCareInstructions','bathingInstructions','activityInstructions'],statuses:[['clipboard','Learn, then practise','Ask your nurse to watch you do the routine yourself before you do it alone at home.'],['droplet','Water needs a plan','Showering, bathing, and swimming carry different risks. Get advice for each one separately.'],['leaf','Make room for daily life','Talk about your actual work, hobbies, and exercise so the plan fits the life you have.']],caregiver:'With permission, help look at the site — a second pair of eyes catches change early. Wash your hands first, and only carry out tasks the PD team has taught you.',question:'When is it okay to start showering?',answers:['When my PD team says the site is ready','As soon as I get home'],correct:0,yes:'That’s right. Your team will tell you when the site has healed enough and how to protect it.',no:'A new site needs to stay dry. Ask your team when it is ready and how to cover it.'},
        {name:'When to call your team',sub:'Know the warning signs',category:'Getting help',title:'Notice a change? Make the call.',description:'You will know your own exit site better than anyone. The two comparisons below show what a change looks like. If you see any of these, call your PD team immediately, even if you feel completely well and even if you are unsure.',visual:'warnings',statuses:[['phone','Call right away','Do not wait for your next visit and do not wait to see whether it settles overnight.'],['message','Say what has changed','Describe pain, fever, how the exit site looks, and any change in the drained fluid.'],['shield','If you cannot reach them','Seek urgent medical care. Use your local emergency service for severe illness.']],caregiver:'Help make the call and describe what has changed. Cloudy drained fluid needs an immediate call even when your loved one feels completely well.',question:'The drained fluid looks cloudy, but I feel fine. What now?',answers:['Wait until tomorrow to see if it clears','Call my PD team immediately'],correct:1,yes:'Yes. Cloudy fluid can be the first sign of infection and often appears before any pain. Call immediately.',no:'Please do not wait. Cloudy fluid needs an immediate call even if you feel well — it can be the earliest sign of infection.'}
      ]
